<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Romania</h1><p class="st-meta">1989 CE – present · capital Bucharest</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Present-day Romania, a democracy since the revolution of 1989 and a member of NATO and the European Union.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The revolution of December 1989 overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu">Nicolae Ceaușescu</a> and ended the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/socialist-republic-of-romania">Socialist Republic of Romania</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-salvation-front-romania">National Salvation Front</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ion-iliescu">Ion Iliescu</a>, took power and won the elections of May 1990, though violent interventions by miners in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> in 1990 cast doubt on its commitment to democracy. A new constitution was adopted in 1991.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a> is a semi-presidential republic with a directly elected president and a bicameral parliament. Power first passed peacefully to the opposition in 1996 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emil-constantinescu">Emil Constantinescu</a>. Privatisation and reform were slow in the 1990s, but growth accelerated in the 2000s, driven by industry, services and IT. Millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> emigrated to work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-romania">Romania</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 2004 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 2007. Anti-corruption campaigns and mass protests in 2017 shaped politics, and the annulment of the 2024 presidential election over alleged foreign interference caused a political crisis before a rerun in 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/romania.jpg" alt="Romania" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AdiJapan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1989 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bucharest</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ion Iliescu, Traian Băsescu, Klaus Iohannis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Romanian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romanian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Romania</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=romania" aria-label="Romania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5304 607 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Romania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5624 863L5624 863"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5624 863h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5624 863h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bucharest. <a href="/atlas?year=2008&amp;focus=romania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
